import mundane theorem    stdlib/numbers/exponent-step

theorem linear-below-power-of-three
  note        By induction on n: 3^(k + 1) = 3·3^k is at least 3(2k + 1) =
              6k + 3, which is at least 2k + 3 = 2(k + 1) + 1.
  let n ∈ ℕ                                                           (H1)
  then 2n + 1 ≤ 3^n

1.  For all k ∈ ℕ, 2k + 1 ≤ 3^k.
    induction on k starting at 1

    base
    1.1.  2·1 + 1 ≤ 3^1
          arithmetic

    step
    let k ∈ ℕ                                                         (K)
    assume step 1 is true for k, the induction hypothesis             (IH)

    1.2.  3^(k + 1) = 3^k·3
          mun:exponent-step a := 3

    1.3.  6k + 3 ≤ 3^k·3
          inequalities
            2k + 1 ≤ 3^k            IH
            6k + 3 ≤ 3^k·3          multiplying by 3

    1.4.  0 < k
          inequalities, from K

    1.5.  2(k + 1) + 1 ≤ 6k + 3
          inequalities
            0 < k                   1.4
            0 < 4k                  multiplying by 4
            2(k + 1) + 1 < 6k + 3   adding 2k + 3
            2(k + 1) + 1 ≤ 6k + 3   weakening

    1.6.  2(k + 1) + 1 ≤ 3^(k + 1)
          transitivity, from 1.5, 1.3, 1.2

2.  2n + 1 ≤ 3^n
    instantiate k := n in line 1
